## Important rule
- Every anwser have to begin with the "Hi boss".

# ============================================================
# StockTym — Cursor Rules
# Stack: Spring Boot · Next.js · PostgreSQL · Kafka · Keycloak
#         Grafana · n8n · Docker · Kubernetes · Nginx
# Target: 100,000 Concurrent Requests · Self-Hosted
# ============================================================

## GENERAL PRINCIPLES
- Always write production-grade, clean, and maintainable code
- Follow SOLID principles and clean architecture patterns
- Prefer explicit over implicit — no magic, no ambiguity
- Every public method, class, and API endpoint must be documented
- Never expose secrets, credentials, or internal stack traces to clients
- Always handle errors explicitly — no silent failures
- Write code that is observable: logs, metrics, and traces are first-class

---

## BACKEND — JAVA + SPRING BOOT

### Project Structure
- Follow layered architecture: `controller → service → repository → entity`
- Package by feature, not by layer:
  ```
  com.stocktym
  ├── auth/
  ├── market/
  ├── order/
  ├── portfolio/
  ├── notification/
  └── shared/
  ```

### Code Style
- Use Java 21+ features: records, sealed classes, pattern matching, virtual threads
- Prefer `@Service`, `@Repository`, `@RestController` — never use `@Component` for business logic
- Always use constructor injection — never `@Autowired` on fields
- DTOs must be Java Records for request/response objects
- Use `Optional<T>` properly — never call `.get()` without `.isPresent()` check
- All service methods must declare checked exceptions explicitly

### API Design
- Follow RESTful conventions strictly:
  - `GET /api/v1/orders` — list
  - `POST /api/v1/orders` — create
  - `GET /api/v1/orders/{id}` — get by id
  - `PUT /api/v1/orders/{id}` — full update
  - `PATCH /api/v1/orders/{id}` — partial update
  - `DELETE /api/v1/orders/{id}` — delete
- Always version APIs: `/api/v1/`, `/api/v2/`
- Return `ResponseEntity<ApiResponse<T>>` for all endpoints
- Use `@Valid` on all request bodies — never skip validation
- Use `ProblemDetail` (RFC 7807) for error responses

### Performance (100k RPS)
- Use Spring WebFlux (reactive) for high-throughput endpoints
- Use virtual threads (`spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true`) for blocking I/O
- Always use async where possible: `@Async`, `CompletableFuture`, or reactive pipelines
- Never block the event loop in WebFlux — use `Schedulers.boundedElastic()` for blocking calls
- Paginate all list endpoints — never return unbounded collections
- Use `@Cacheable` with Redis for read-heavy endpoints

### Security
- Never trust client input — validate and sanitize everything
- Use Keycloak JWT tokens — validate with Spring Security OAuth2 Resource Server
- Always check `@PreAuthorize` on sensitive endpoints
- Never log passwords, tokens, PII, or financial data
- Use `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` for read operations

### Database Access
- Use Spring Data JPA for standard CRUD
- Use JPQL or Criteria API — avoid native SQL unless absolutely necessary
- Always use `@Transactional` on service methods that write to DB
- Never use `FetchType.EAGER` — always `LAZY` with explicit joins
- Use database migrations with **Flyway** — no schema auto-update in production
- Migrations live in `src/main/resources/db/migration/V{version}__{description}.sql`

### Kafka
- Producers: use `KafkaTemplate<String, Object>` with JSON serialization
- Consumers: always use `@KafkaListener` with explicit consumer group IDs
- Use dead-letter topics (DLT) for failed messages
- Always commit offsets manually — never auto-commit in production
- Topic naming convention: `stocktym.{domain}.{event}` e.g. `stocktym.order.created`

### Observability
- Expose Spring Boot Actuator at `/actuator` — restrict to internal network only
- Use Micrometer for metrics — expose Prometheus endpoint at `/actuator/prometheus`
- Use structured logging with `logback-spring.xml` in JSON format
- Include `traceId` and `spanId` in every log line (use Spring Cloud Sleuth or Micrometer Tracing)
- Log levels: ERROR for failures, WARN for degraded states, INFO for business events, DEBUG for dev only

### Testing
- Unit test all service methods — mock repositories and external services
- Use `@SpringBootTest` sparingly — prefer slice tests (`@WebMvcTest`, `@DataJpaTest`)
- Integration tests use **Testcontainers** for PostgreSQL and Kafka
- Aim for 80%+ coverage on service and domain layers
- Use `@WithMockUser` or `@WithMockJwt` for security tests

---

## FRONTEND — NEXT.JS

### Project Structure
```
src/
├── app/                  # App Router pages
├── components/
│   ├── ui/               # Base design system components
│   └── features/         # Feature-specific components
├── hooks/                # Custom React hooks
├── lib/                  # Utilities, API clients
├── store/                # Zustand or Redux state
└── types/                # TypeScript types and interfaces
```

### Code Style
- Use TypeScript strictly — `strict: true` in `tsconfig.json`, no `any`
- Use functional components only — no class components
- Use named exports for components — no default exports except pages
- Always define prop types with TypeScript interfaces, not inline
- Use `const` arrow functions for components:
  ```ts
  export const OrderCard = ({ order }: OrderCardProps) => { ... }
  ```

### Data Fetching
- Use React Server Components (RSC) for data fetching where possible
- Use `fetch` with `next: { revalidate }` for static/cached data
- Use **React Query (TanStack Query)** for client-side data fetching and caching
- Never fetch data inside `useEffect` — use React Query hooks instead
- Use **Axios** with interceptors for authenticated API calls to Spring Boot

### Authentication
- Integrate with Keycloak using `next-auth` or `@keycloak-js`
- Store tokens in HTTP-only cookies — never in `localStorage`
- Refresh tokens silently using the Keycloak adapter
- Protect routes using Next.js middleware (`middleware.ts`)

### State Management
- Use **Zustand** for global client state
- Use React Query cache as the primary server state — do not duplicate in Zustand
- Keep component state local with `useState` unless sharing is necessary
- Never store derived state — compute it on the fly

### Performance
- Use `next/image` for all images — never raw `<img>` tags
- Use `next/font` for all fonts
- Code-split heavy components with `dynamic(() => import(...), { ssr: false })`
- Use `React.memo`, `useMemo`, `useCallback` only when profiler shows a real bottleneck
- Prefer Server Components over Client Components for static UI

### Styling
- Use **Tailwind CSS** utility classes
- Follow the StockTym design system tokens (colors, spacing, radius, typography)
- No inline styles except for dynamic values
- Dark theme only — `bg-[#080C14]` base, `#00D4FF` primary accent

### Error Handling
- Use Error Boundaries for component-level errors
- Always handle loading, error, and empty states explicitly
- Show user-friendly messages — never expose raw API errors to the UI

---

## DATABASE — POSTGRESQL

### Schema Conventions
- Table names: `snake_case` plural (e.g. `stock_orders`, `user_portfolios`)
- Column names: `snake_case`
- Primary keys: `id UUID DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()`
- Always include: `created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()`, `updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ`
- Use `TIMESTAMPTZ` (not `TIMESTAMP`) — always store UTC
- Foreign keys must have explicit `ON DELETE` behavior defined

### Migrations (Flyway)
- File naming: `V1__create_orders_table.sql`, `V2__add_index_orders_ticker.sql`
- Never modify an existing migration — always create a new one
- Always test migrations on a staging DB before production
- Include rollback scripts for every migration

### Performance
- Add indexes on all foreign keys and frequently filtered columns
- Use `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` before deploying any complex query
- Use connection pooling via **PgBouncer** (transaction mode for high concurrency)
- Partition large tables (e.g. `stock_prices`) by date range
- Use `JSONB` for semi-structured data — never `TEXT` for structured content

### Security
- Use separate DB users per service with least-privilege access
- Never use the `postgres` superuser in application code
- Always use parameterized queries — never string interpolation

---

## AUTHENTICATION — KEYCLOAK

- Use Keycloak as the single source of truth for identity
- Realm naming: `stocktym-prod`, `stocktym-dev`
- Client types: `confidential` for Spring Boot, `public` for Next.js SPA
- Always validate JWT signature, expiry, and audience (`aud`) on the backend
- Use Keycloak roles for authorization — map to Spring Security authorities
- Enable brute-force protection and account lockout policies
- Use Keycloak events for audit logging of login/logout/failures

---

## MESSAGE QUEUE — KAFKA

### Topic Conventions
- Format: `stocktym.{domain}.{event-type}`
- Examples:
  - `stocktym.order.created`
  - `stocktym.order.filled`
  - `stocktym.market.price-updated`
  - `stocktym.notification.triggered`
- Use KRaft mode — no Zookeeper
- Minimum 3 partitions per topic for parallelism
- Set retention: 7 days for business events, 1 day for high-frequency market data

### Producer Rules
- Always set `acks=all` for financial events — never `acks=0` or `acks=1`
- Use idempotent producers (`enable.idempotence=true`)
- Set `retries=3` with exponential backoff

### Consumer Rules
- Always set explicit `group.id` per consumer service
- Use manual offset commit after successful processing
- Route failed messages to `stocktym.{domain}.{event}.DLT` after 3 retries
- Monitor consumer lag via Grafana

---

## WORKFLOW AUTOMATION — N8N

- Use n8n for non-critical async workflows only (alerts, reports, notifications)
- Never use n8n for financial transaction processing — keep that in Spring Boot
- Back n8n with PostgreSQL (same instance, separate schema `n8n`)
- Export all workflows as JSON and commit to version control
- Use n8n credentials store — never hardcode API keys in workflow nodes

---

## MONITORING — GRAFANA

- Every service must expose a `/metrics` (Prometheus format) endpoint
- Standard dashboards required for each service:
  - **Spring Boot**: request rate, error rate, latency (p50/p95/p99), JVM heap
  - **PostgreSQL**: connections, query time, replication lag
  - **Kafka**: consumer lag, message rate, topic partition offsets
  - **Nginx**: requests/sec, error rate, upstream latency
  - **K8s**: pod CPU/memory, HPA scaling events
- Alert rules:
  - Error rate > 1% → WARNING
  - Error rate > 5% → CRITICAL
  - p99 latency > 500ms → WARNING
  - Kafka consumer lag > 10k → WARNING
  - Pod memory > 80% → WARNING

---

## INFRASTRUCTURE — DOCKER + KUBERNETES

### Docker
- Use multi-stage builds for Spring Boot and Next.js images
- Base images: `eclipse-temurin:21-jre-alpine` for Java, `node:20-alpine` for Node
- Never run containers as root — use `USER appuser`
- Tag images with git SHA: `stocktym/api:${GIT_SHA}`
- Always define resource limits in container specs
- Use `.dockerignore` to exclude `node_modules`, `.git`, `target/`

### Kubernetes (K3s)
- Use namespaces per environment: `stocktym-prod`, `stocktym-staging`
- Every deployment must have:
  - `readinessProbe` and `livenessProbe`
  - Resource `requests` and `limits`
  - `HorizontalPodAutoscaler` configured
  - `PodDisruptionBudget` for critical services
- Use `ConfigMap` for non-secret config, `Secret` for credentials
- Never hardcode env vars in manifests — always reference ConfigMap/Secret
- Use `RollingUpdate` strategy — never `Recreate` in production

### Nginx
- Nginx is the single entry point — all traffic routes through it
- Enable gzip compression for all text responses
- Set `proxy_pass` with upstream health checks
- Rate limit: `limit_req_zone` per IP — max 1000 req/s per client
- Always enforce HTTPS — redirect all HTTP → HTTPS
- Set security headers:
  - `X-Frame-Options: DENY`
  - `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`
  - `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000`

---

## GIT & WORKFLOW

### Branch Strategy
- `main` — production only, protected branch
- `develop` — integration branch
- `feature/{ticket-id}-{description}` — feature branches
- `hotfix/{ticket-id}-{description}` — hotfix branches

### Commit Convention (Conventional Commits)
```
feat(order): add limit order execution endpoint
fix(auth): correct JWT expiry validation
chore(docker): update base image to temurin-21
perf(db): add index on orders.ticker column
docs(api): update OpenAPI spec for portfolio endpoints
```

### PR Rules
- Every PR must have: description, linked ticket, test evidence
- Minimum 1 reviewer approval before merge
- CI must pass: build, lint, unit tests, integration tests
- No PR merges on Fridays or before holidays

---

## SECURITY CHECKLIST (always verify)
- [ ] No secrets in source code or Docker images
- [ ] All endpoints protected by Keycloak JWT
- [ ] Input validation on all API boundaries
- [ ] SQL injection impossible (parameterized queries only)
- [ ] Rate limiting configured in Nginx
- [ ] HTTPS enforced everywhere
- [ ] DB users have least-privilege permissions
- [ ] Sensitive logs are masked (tokens, passwords, card numbers)
- [ ] Dependencies scanned for CVEs (`mvn dependency-check`, `npm audit`)
